---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { href } from "../data/site";
import { a0 } from "../data/a0_results";
const k = (x: number) => Math.round(x).toLocaleString("en-AU");
const f1 = (x: number) => x.toFixed(1);
const f2 = (x: number) => x.toFixed(2);
const pct = Math.round(a0.loss_reduction_vs_naive_pct);
const fig = (name: string) => href(`/figures/a0/${name}.png`);
const figw = (name: string) => href(`/figures/a0/${name}.webp`);
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "ScholarlyArticle",
  name: "Study A0 — N that pays, where it pays, and where to look next",
  description: "A controlled recovery study on a synthetic barley belt: the orchestra refuses the crop model where the field disagrees and halves the realised loss against a model-only recommendation.",
  author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Max Moldovan", identifier: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9680-8474" },
  datePublished: "2026-08-26",
};
---
<Base title="Study A0 — results" description="Study A0 on a synthetic barley belt: the orchestra refuses the crop model where the field disagrees, prices the decision, and cuts realised loss by about half against a model-only recommendation. Every number is bound to the study's artefacts." path="/a0" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="kicker">Study A0 · digital-twin arm · version 0.2 · 2026-08-26</p>
      <h1>N that pays, where it pays, and where to look next</h1>
      <p class="prose lead mt-2">For each site-season of a barley belt: which nitrogen rate maximises
        expected profit net of nitrogen and its nitrous-oxide cost, how confident can we be, where should
        the crop model be refused, and what is one soil test or one strip trial worth? This page reports the
        first executed study of the series. It is a controlled recovery exercise on a synthetic belt with a
        known truth, not a field result, and it says so wherever the distinction matters.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-3">The set-up</h2>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li><strong>Synthetic Barley Belt.</strong> {a0.n_sites} sites on real grain-belt coordinates with real
        climate (NASA POWER) and soil (SoilGrids), five seasons, six nitrogen rates, three varieties, three
        replicates, yields simulated by APSIM Next Gen.</li>
      <li><strong>The planted truth.</strong> At {a0.n_constrained_sites} sites a hidden subsoil constraint caps
        yield and flattens the response above 60 kg N/ha. The simulator used for inversion is never told.</li>
      <li><strong>The chain.</strong> apsimR (truth) → quorum surrogate and PESTO inversion (the
        mechanism lens) → kernR TACI (does the field agree with the mechanism?) → a plateau-capable
        field-record lens where TACI refuses → decideR (loss, EVPI, EVSI) → optimix (which sites to test) →
        grainPlan (the grower-facing plan). Every hand-off is a typed manifest; every refusal is a typed
        object read downstream.</li>
      <li><strong>The economics.</strong> Barley at {f1(a0.price_barley_aud_t)} AUD/t, nitrogen at {f2(a0.cost_per_kgn_aud)} AUD/kg N plus an IPCC-default nitrous-oxide shadow price.</li>
      <li><strong>The discipline.</strong> Six independent oracles run inside the stage scripts, the report
        binds every number to a computed object, and the whole chain is re-verified on a re-seeded belt.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-3">Three numbers</h2>
    <div class="studies">
      <article class="card">
        <p class="kicker">Realised loss against the planted truth</p>
        <h3>{k(a0.loss_cascade_total_aud_ha)} vs {k(a0.loss_naive_total_aud_ha)} AUD/ha</h3>
        <p class="prose">Summed over 198 site-seasons, orchestra cascade vs the naive mechanism-only
          recommendation: <strong>{pct} per cent less loss</strong>. At the constrained cells {f1(a0.loss_cascade_constrained_mean)} vs {f1(a0.loss_naive_constrained_mean)} AUD/ha per site-season; at the unconstrained cells {f1(a0.loss_cascade_unconstrained_mean)} vs {f1(a0.loss_naive_unconstrained_mean)}. The ordering reproduces on a re-seeded belt
          ({k(a0.rerun_loss_cascade_total)} vs {k(a0.rerun_loss_naive_total)}).</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <p class="kicker">Nitrogen and emissions</p>
        <h3>{k(a0.n_saved_vs_naive_kgn)} kg N not applied · {f1(a0.co2e_saved_vs_naive_t_ha)} t CO₂-e/ha avoided</h3>
        <p class="prose">The field record, read through a lens that can hold a plateau, locates the
          constraint the model cannot see: the posterior interval of the plateau onset contains the planted
          60 kg N/ha at {a0.plateau_interval_covers_60_n} of {a0.n_constrained_sites} constrained sites
          (median onset {Math.round(a0.x0_median_constrained)} vs {Math.round(a0.x0_median_unconstrained)} kg N/ha elsewhere). TACI refuses the mechanism with sensitivity {f2(a0.taci_sensitivity)} and
          specificity {f2(a0.taci_specificity)}.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <p class="kicker">Where the next research dollar pays</p>
        <h3>{a0.n_evsi_priced} of {a0.n_sites} sites priced · top site {Math.round(a0.evsi_top_aud_ha)} AUD/ha</h3>
        <p class="prose">The expected value of sample information of one strip trial or one soil nitrate
          test, per site — a map of where the next test pays. Directional, not calibrated: the
          importance-weighted estimate is effective-sample-size limited at this posterior size, and the
          information prices are unverified inputs.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-3">The four panels</h2>
    <figure class="mt-1">
      <picture><source srcset={figw("fig_poster_panels")} type="image/webp" /><img src={fig("fig_poster_panels")} width="1600" height="1080" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        alt="Four panels. A: a map of the forty sites on real grain-belt coordinates coloured by the lens the cascade used, with the constrained sites ringed. B: yield-response curves at four example sites showing the naive, cascade and truth-optimal rates. C: bar charts of mean realised loss by policy, split by whether the constraint was planted. D: a scatter of growing-season rainfall against clay content coloured by the expected value of sample information." /></picture>
      <figcaption class="muted">A: where the mechanism was refused and the field record took over. B: what each lens recommends at four sites chosen by a stated rule. C: what each policy loses against the planted truth. D: where the next research dollar pays.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="mt-2">
      <picture><source srcset={figw("fig_stat_curves")} type="image/webp" /><img src={fig("fig_stat_curves")} width="1600" height="1028" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        alt="Forty small panels, one per site, each showing the observed plot-mean yields at six nitrogen rates with the posterior mean and ninety per cent band of the field-record lens; constrained sites flatten at sixty kilograms of nitrogen per hectare." /></picture>
      <figcaption class="muted">The field-record lens per site: a leave-one-out weighted mixture of linear-plateau and quadratic-plateau random curves over the observed means. This is the posterior the decision layer uses wherever the mechanism is refused.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="studies mt-2">
      <figure>
        <picture><source srcset={figw("fig_policy_ledger")} type="image/webp" /><img src={fig("fig_policy_ledger")} width="1600" height="943" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
          alt="Bar chart of mean realised loss per site-season for four policies, naive, abstain-to-zero, abstain-to-status-quo and the cascade, split into no-constraint and constraint-planted cells; the cascade is lowest or near-lowest in both." /></picture>
        <figcaption class="muted">What each fallback convention costs, and where.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <picture><source srcset={figw("fig_plateau_recovery")} type="image/webp" /><img src={fig("fig_plateau_recovery")} width="1600" height="960" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
          alt="Per-site posterior median and ninety per cent interval of the plateau onset, unconstrained sites on the left and constrained sites on the right, with a dashed line at the planted sixty kilograms of nitrogen per hectare." /></picture>
        <figcaption class="muted">Plateau onset recovered from the field record, against the planted plateau at the constrained sites.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-3">What it does not show</h2>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li>A synthetic belt, not a real one. The study speaks to shape (which sites respond, where a constraint flattens
        the curve), not to real-world dollar magnitudes.</li>
      <li>Two of the six stage oracles fail and are reported as failures: the plateau-onset separation between
        constrained and unconstrained sites (unconstrained sites plateau too, so onset alone conflates "has a
        plateau" with "has the constraint"), and an under-powered site-level test on the re-seeded belt.</li>
      <li>Version 0.1 of this study lost to the naive arm: its second lens was linear in rate and could only
        recommend a corner of the candidate grid. The finding was reported before the lens was changed; the
        oracles were not.</li>
      <li>The value-of-information figures are directional, and the information prices, the district status-quo
        rate and the carbon price are unverified inputs, stated as such.</li>
      <li>No GRDC-origin data enters the belt, the figures or this page.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-3">Reproduce it</h2>
    <p class="prose">The study is a driver over seven stage scripts, each a standalone R pipeline with its
      own oracle. Read them in this order:</p>
    <ol class="prose">
      <li><strong>Generate the belt.</strong> Sample {a0.n_sites} sites on real grain-belt coordinates, collect NASA POWER
        climate and SoilGrids soil, run APSIM barley for every site, season, variety and rate, then plant the hidden
        subsoil constraint at {a0.n_constrained_sites} sites after the simulator has spoken.</li>
      <li><strong>Invert and test.</strong> Train a fast emulator of the simulator per site-season, calibrate it with
        PESTO on the low rates (0 to 60 kg N/ha) and let kernR test, on the higher rates, whether the field record
        still agrees with the calibrated model.</li>
      <li><strong>Fit the field record.</strong> Fit linear-plateau and quadratic-plateau curves with brms on all
        plots, weight them by leave-one-out predictive fit, and carry the per-site yield posterior forward.</li>
      <li><strong>Decide and value.</strong> Price every candidate rate by expected profit with decideR, using the
        model where kernR accepted it and the field-record fit where it did not; price one soil test and one strip
        trial per site; write the grainPlan nitrogen plan.</li>
      <li><strong>Report.</strong> Bind every number on this page and in the report to a computed object.</li>
      <li><strong>Re-verify.</strong> Run the whole chain again on a re-seeded belt.</li>
      <li><strong>Figure and summary.</strong> Draw the four panels and write the binding table of poster numbers.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="prose">The scripts are written in the house pipeline style and commented stage by stage. They are
      released as one archive with a README that lists what each stage reads and writes, alongside the study tree.
      The generated data are not shipped: stage 01 regenerates them from the public sources named above.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-3">Take it with you</h2>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li><a href={href("/downloads/a0_poster.pdf")}>The symposium poster</a> (PDF, A0 portrait, version 2 of 2026-09-30; every number bound to the same table as this page).</li>
      <li><a href={href("/downloads/orchestra_brief.pdf")}>A one-page briefing note</a> (PDF) for research managers and data custodians.</li>
      <li><a href={href("/downloads/a0_summary.md")}>The one-page study summary</a> (Markdown).</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/studies")}>The study series</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/members")}>The members</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/governance")}>Governance</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
